WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.931
Somewhere in a cloud billing dashboard, one query is quietly burning fifteen hundred dollars a day.

00:00:04.931 --> 00:00:07.863
A weekly trending genres report.

00:00:07.863 --> 00:00:10.795
It runs a hundred times a day, nine hours a run.

00:00:10.795 --> 00:00:13.726
By year end it has spent about five hundred fifty thousand dollars.

00:00:13.726 --> 00:00:16.657
Half a million, for one report.

00:00:16.657 --> 00:00:19.589
Nothing is broken.

00:00:19.589 --> 00:00:22.521
The answer is correct every time.

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:25.452
So where does the money go.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:29.081
Before you optimize, you measure.

00:00:29.081 --> 00:00:32.711
So we lay out the physical schema.

00:00:32.711 --> 00:00:36.340
The report joins two tables.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.970
Songs is four hundred pages, about twenty five gigabytes.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.599
Listens is sixteen thousand pages, the full terabyte, ten billion rows.

00:00:43.599 --> 00:00:47.229
The naive plan is a Hash Partition Join across the whole thing.

00:00:47.229 --> 00:00:50.858
Three passes over both tables, about forty nine thousand reads every run.

00:00:50.858 --> 00:00:54.488
And here is the cruel part.

00:00:54.488 --> 00:00:58.117
This week's window, October twenty first through twenty seventh, lives in just a hundred and fifty of those pages.

00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:01.747
One percent.

00:01:01.747 --> 00:01:05.376
The buffer pool holds twenty pages at a time.

00:01:05.376 --> 00:01:09.006
A hundred and fifty pages we want, sixteen thousand pages we read.

00:01:09.006 --> 00:01:12.635
The whole story is in that ratio.

00:01:12.635 --> 00:01:17.003
Now watch the bill come down in stages.

00:01:17.003 --> 00:01:21.371
Stage one, the red baseline.

00:01:21.371 --> 00:01:25.739
The Hash Partition Join scans all of it, every column, forty nine thousand reads, five hundred fifty thousand dollars a year.

00:01:25.739 --> 00:01:30.107
The disaster.

00:01:30.107 --> 00:01:34.475
Stage two, add a B plus tree index on listen time.

00:01:34.475 --> 00:01:38.843
The index jumps straight to this week and skips the rest, so you scan fewer rows.

00:01:38.843 --> 00:01:43.211
Ninety times cheaper, five hundred fifty four reads.

00:01:43.211 --> 00:01:47.579
Stage three, switch to columnar storage.

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:51.947
Each column lives in its own pages, so you read only the four columns the query touches and ignore the rest.

00:01:51.947 --> 00:01:56.314
Fewer columns read, nine times cheaper again.

00:01:56.314 --> 00:02:00.682
Stage four, compress those columns.

00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:05.050
Dictionary encoding for the genres, bit packing for the ratings, run length encoding for the sorted user IDs.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:09.418
Fewer bytes stored, twice cheaper, and the bar turns green at about four hundred dollars a year.

00:02:09.418 --> 00:02:13.786
Each win attacks a different kind of waste, so they stack.

00:02:13.786 --> 00:02:18.154
The index cut rows, columnar cut columns, compression cut bytes.

00:02:18.154 --> 00:02:22.522
Three orthogonal fixes, so they multiply instead of add.

00:02:22.522 --> 00:02:26.890
Ninety plus nine plus two would be a hundred and one times.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:31.258
Ninety times nine times two is roughly fourteen hundred times overall.

00:02:31.258 --> 00:02:36.979
One unindexed weekly report can cost half a million dollars a year.

00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:42.701
An index, a columnar layout, and compression each attack a separate kind of waste, so stacking them multiplies the savings instead of adding them.

00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:48.422
The same query, the same answer, fourteen hundred times cheaper.

00:02:48.422 --> 00:02:54.144
When you find a query that costs too much, do not hunt for one magic fix.

00:02:54.144 --> 00:02:59.865
Find the independent kinds of waste, and remove them one at a time.
